helpMouseBody.message:
Snow reacts to the pointer within the configured radius:
• slow movement gently pushes flakes aside, fast movement carries them in an air stream;
• **left button** — an explosion that scatters the snow;
• **right button** — pulls the snow towards the cursor.
Snow never blocks clicks: the page stays fully usable.

helpEffectsBody.message:
The **API** tab has effect buttons on top of the current snowfall:
• **Snow burst** — a dense wave of flakes from the top;
• **Strong wind** — gusty wind for 10 seconds;
• **Clear effects** — removes temporary layers without stopping the main snow.
Up to four temporary layers run at once; they fade out smoothly when they end.

helpObsBody.message:
Snow can be layered over a stream as a transparent **Browser** source:
• start the relay, enter the token and press **OBS link** on the **API** tab;
• in OBS add a **Browser** source, paste the link and set the size, e.g. 1920 × 1080;
• options go before `#token=`, e.g. `?snowmax=120&renderer=3d`;
• the `let-it-snow-relay.lua` script (Tools → Scripts) starts the relay together with OBS and adds the source to the scene.

helpHttpBody.message:
While the relay is running, any program on this computer can control the snow:
• `POST http://127.0.0.1:8787/api/command` with the header `Authorization: Bearer <token>` and JSON `{"command": "burst", "params": {"count": 400}}`;
• commands: `burst`, `wind`, `glyphs`, `gif`, `start`, `stop`, `clearEffects`;
• a `202` response means the command was accepted; `delivered` is the number of connected clients;
• a WebSocket `/ws` is available too. All parameters and limits are described in the External API reference.
